 "text": "## Step 1: Calculate the distillate flow rate D\nTo find the distillate flow rate D, we need to consider the fractions of the key components that are recovered in the distillate and bottoms. For n-butane, 98% is recovered in the distillate, and for n-pentane, 3% is recovered in the distillate (100% - 97%). We'll assume all the propane goes to the distillate and all the n-hexane to the bottoms.\n\nLet's denote the distillate flow rate as D, the bottoms flow rate as B, and the feed flow rate as F. We know that F = 100 kmol/h.\n\nThe fractions of n-butane and n-pentane in the distillate are 0.98z_F (n-butane) and 0.03z_F (n-pentane), respectively.\n\nThe fractions of n-butane and n-pentane in the bottoms are 0.01z_F (n-butane) and 0.97z_F (n-pentane), respectively.\n\n## Step 2: Calculate the distillate flow rate D\nWe can set up the following equation to find the distillate flow rate D:\n\nD = F(0.98z_F + 0.03z_F) + F(0.01z_F + 0.97z_F)\u03b1_HK\n\nSimplifying the equation:\n\nD = F(0.98z_F + 0.03z_F) + F(0.01z_F + 0.97z_F)\n\nSince \u03b1_HK is not given for n-butane, we assume it's the relative volatility of n-butane, which is 2.0.\n\nD = F(0.98z_F + 0.03z_F) + F(0.01z_F + 0.97z_F)2.0\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\nD = 100(0.98*0.35 + 0.03*0.40) + 100(0.01*0.40 + 0.97*0.40)2.0\n\nD = 100(0.343 + 0.012) + 100(0.004 + 0.976)2.0\n\nD = 100(0.355) + 100(0.980)2.0\n\nD = 35.5 + 1960\n\nD = 1995.5 kmol/h\n\n## Step 3: Calculate the minimum number of theoretical stages N_min (Fenske equation)\nThe Fenske equation is used to find the minimum number of theoretical stages N_min.\n\nN_min = ln(\u03b1_HK/\u03b1_LK) / ln(\u03b1_HK)\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\nN_min = ln(2.0/1.0) / ln(2.0)\n\nN_min = ln(2) / ln(2)\n\nN_min = 0.693 / 0.693\n\nN_min = 1\n\n## Step 4: Calculate the root \u03b8 of the Underwood feed equation\nThe Underwood feed equation is used to find the root \u03b8.\n\n\u03b8 = (ln(\u03b1_HK/\u03b1_LK) - ln(\u03b1_LK/\u03b1_HK)) / (ln(\u03b1_HK) - ln(\u03b1_LK))\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\n\u03b8 = (ln(2.0/1.0) - ln(1.0/2.0)) / (ln(2.0) - ln(1.0))\n\n\u03b8 = (0.693 - (-0.693)) / (0.693 - 0)\n\n\u03b8 = 1.386 / 0.693\n\n\u03b8 = 2\n\n## Step 5: Calculate the minimum reflux ratio R_min\nThe minimum reflux ratio R_min can be found using the Fenske equation.\n\nR_min = (\u03b1_HK - 1) / (\u03b1_HK - \u03b1_LK)\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\nR_min = (2.0 - 1) / (2.0 - 1)\n\nR_min = 1\n\n## Step 6: Calculate the number of theoretical stages N using the Gilliland correlation\nThe Gilliland correlation is used to find the number of theoretical stages N.\n\nY = 0.75(1 - X^0.5668)\n\nX = (R - R_min) / (R + 1)\n\nY = (N - N_min) / (N + 1)\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\nX = (1.3 - 1) / (1.3 + 1)\n\nX = 0.3 / 2.3\n\nX = 0.1304\n\nY = 0.75(1 - 0.1304^0.5668)\n\nY = 0.75(1 - 0.0405)\n\nY = 0.7595\n\n(N - N_min) / (N + 1) = 0.7595\n\nN - N_min = 0.7595N + 0.7595\n\nN - 1 = 0.7595N + 0.7595\n\n0.2405N = 0.7595\n\nN = 0.7595 / 0.2405\n\nN = 3.162\n\n## Step 7: Calculate the ratio N_R/N_S of rectifying to stripping stages from the Kirkbride equation\nThe Kirkbride equation is used to find the ratio N_R/N_S.\n\nlog\u2081\u2080(N_R/N_S) = 0.206 log\u2081\u2080[(z_HK/z_LK)(x_B,LK/x_D,HK)\u00b2(B/D)]\n\nSubstituting the values:\n\nlog\u2081\u2080(N_R/N_S) = 0.206 log\u2081\u2080[(0.35/0.40)(0.98/0.03)\u00b2(0.01/0.99)\u00b2(1960/1995.5)]\n\nlog\u2081\u2080(N_R/N_S) = 0.206 log\u2081\u2080[(0.875)(32.26)\u00b2(0.0101)\u00b2(0.986)]\n\nlog\u2081\u2080(N_R/N_S) = 0.206 log\u2081\u2080[(0.875)(1041.14)\u00b2(0.0001041)\u00b2(0.986)]\n\nlog\u2081\u2080(N_R/N_S) = 0.206 log\u2081\u2080[(0.875)(1085.85)\u00b2(0.000108)\u00b2(0.986)]\n\nlog\u2081\u2080(N_R/N_S) = 0.206 log\u2081\u2080[(0.875)(117792.7)\u00b2(0.0000113)\u00b2(0.986)]\n\nlog\u2081\u2080(N_R/N_S) = 0.206 log\u2081\u2080[(0.875)(138.95)\u00b2(0.000012)\u00b2(0.986)]\n\nlog\u2081\u2080(N_R/N_S) = 0.206 log\u2081\u2080[(0.875)(1927.3)\u00b2(0.000014)\u00b2(0.986)]\n\nlog\u2081\